                    "The 1H NMR shows:\n- 1.79 ppm (s, 3H) - one methyl group\n- 1.93 ppm (s, 6H) - two equivalent methyl groups\n\nFrom the HSQC data:\n- 1.79/19.2 ppm: Methyl group (3H)\n- 1.93/22.8 ppm: Methyl group (3H)\n- 1.93/21.4 ppm: Methyl group (3H)\n\nThis confirms we have three methyl groups total.\n\nFrom the 13C NMR:\n- 168.60 ppm: Carboxylic ester carbon (C=O)\n- 143.85 ppm: Aromatic quaternary or sp2 carbon\n- 130.30 ppm: Aromatic quaternary carbon\n- 22.80, 21.35, 19.20 ppm: Three methyl groups\n\nThe aromatic carbons are at 143.85 and 130.30 ppm, both quaternary (no attached hydrogens). The absence of aromatic protons in the 1H NMR indicates that either all aromatic carbons are quaternary, or that there are no aromatic carbons at all.\n\nA benzene ring has 6 carbons. Given the fact that we see only 2 carbon signals at aromatic shifts might indicate that the molecule has no aromatic carbons, and most likely, the carbon atoms in those shifts belong to a double bond.": 1.0,
                    "The 1H NMR shows:\n- 1.79 ppm (s, 3H) - one methyl group\n- 1.93 ppm (s, 6H) - two equivalent methyl groups\n\nFrom the 13C NMR:\n- 168.60 ppm: This could be an aromatic carbon bearing an electron-withdrawing group\n- 143.85 ppm: Aromatic quaternary carbon\n- 130.30 ppm: Aromatic quaternary carbon\n- 22.80, 21.35, 19.20 ppm: Three methyl groups\n\nLooking at the carbon shift ranges, aromatic carbons can appear up to 150-160 ppm for phenols. The signal at 168.60 ppm is at the high end, but considering it's a quaternary carbon (not in HSQC) and we have no aromatic protons, this could be an aromatic carbon directly attached to the ester group.\n\nIf we have a benzene ring with substituents, and considering the three methyl groups plus an ester:\n- The 168.60 ppm signal represents 1 aromatic carbon bearing the ester\n- The 143.85 ppm signal could represent 2 equivalent aromatic carbons bearing methyl groups\n- The 130.30 ppm signal could represent 3 equivalent aromatic carbons\n\nTotal: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 carbons, but wait - the ester carbon itself isn't aromatic.\n\nActually, reconsidering: if the 168.60 is the ester C=O (sp2), and we have a tetrasubstituted benzene, the pattern would give us:\n- 143.85 ppm: 2 carbons\n- 130.30 ppm: 2 carbons\n- Plus 2 more aromatic carbons at similar shifts that overlap.": 0.0
